<reading-questions xml:id="reflections-orthog">
<title>Reflect on your understanding</title>

<introduction><p>
	In addition to the reflection activities below,
	re-read <xref ref="section-orthog-terminology" text="type-global-title" />.
	Be sure you understand each of the new definitions introduced in this chapter,
	and spend some time committing them to memory.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>
	<title>Dot product versus size of angle</title>
	<p>
	Explain how the size of the angle between two vectors (acute, obtuse, or right)
	can be inferred from the value of the dot product of those two vectors.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Geometry of orthogonal projection</title>
	<p>
	State the geometric interpretation of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> in terms of the line through the origin parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Parallel bases for orthogonal projections</title>
	<p> Explain why <m> \uproj{u}{a} = \uproj{u}{b} </m> when <m> \uvec{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{b} </m> are parallel (nonzero) vectors. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Parallel and orthogonal projections</title>

	<introduction><p>
		In each case, explain geometrically
		(that is, without appealing to the algebraic formula for orthogonal projection).
	</p></introduction>

	<task><title> Parallel orthogonal projection </title><p>
		What is the result of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> when <m> \uvec{u} </m> is parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><title> Orthogonal orthogonal projection </title><p>
		What is the result of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> when <m> \uvec{u} </m> is orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m>?
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Parallel and orthogonal components</title>
	<p> Describe the <term>parallel and orthogonal components</term> of a vector <m> \uvec{u} </m> relative to vector <m> \uvec{a} </m>. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Distance to a line</title>
	<p>
	Summarize the procedure to compute the distance in <m> \R^n </m> between a point and a line through the origin.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Normal vectors</title>
	<p>
	Describe the concept of a <term>normal vector</term> for a line in <m> \R^2 </m> or plane in <m> \R^3 </m>,
	and the relationship between the algebraic equation for the line/plane and the normal vector.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Cross product</title>
	<p>
	Describe the concept of the <term>cross product</term> of two non-parallel vectors in <m> \R^3 </m>:
	what it represents geometrically,
	and how to compute it.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Right-hand rule</title>
	<p>
	State the <term>right-hand rule</term> for cross products in <m> \R^3 </m>.
	</p>
</exercise>

</reading-questions>
